      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-06T03:57:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done",
      "parentUuid": "dce1ca4d_61efe824",
      "revId": "67a01027d8467b6bc48d2550a7bc8f22818cfdcd",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "722ce73f_fee47b95",
        "filename": "src/gn/starlark/crates/attr/src/schema.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 141,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003023
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-06T02:54:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For optional `attr.string(values\u003d[...])` or `attr.int(values\u003d[...])` declarations with no explicit default, this branch installs `\"\"` or `0` directly and never checks whether those values are in the allowed set.\n\nWhen the user omits the attribute, `Attr::create` clones this stored default, so the rule sees a value that violates its own values constraint.\n\nShould we validate the implicit default against `allowed` here, or require `mandatory` / an explicit in-range default in that case?",
      "revId": "67a01027d8467b6bc48d2550a7bc8f22818cfdcd",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "877e9b80_04e4c417",
        "filename": "src/gn/starlark/crates/attr/src/schema.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 141,
      "author": {
        "id": 3810482
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-06T03:57:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I did this to match bazel\u0027s behaviour. Bazel does precisely this wierd behaviour.",
      "parentUuid": "722ce73f_fee47b95",
      "revId": "67a01027d8467b6bc48d2550a7bc8f22818cfdcd",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bffbb75d_2dd2b8d6",
        "filename": "src/gn/starlark/crates/attr/src/schema.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 190,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003023
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-06T02:54:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`Single` and `Many` are handled identically here and nothing else branches on the distinction, so `allow_single_file` currently behaves exactly like `allow_files` with no single-file enforcement. Is this WAI?",
      "revId": "67a01027d8467b6bc48d2550a7bc8f22818cfdcd",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "46f92bf2_aa98de15",
        "filename": "src/gn/starlark/crates/attr/src/schema.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 190,
      "author": {
        "id": 3810482
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-07-06T03:57:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this is WAI. Because we have a label, rather than a target, at this phase, we don\u0027t yet know the number of files the target resolves to.\n\nI do allow_single_file validation when creating the ctx object for the rule implementation, because at that point in time we know that the dependencies have resolved, and thus know how many files the label resolves to.",
      "parentUuid": "bffbb75d_2dd2b8d6",
      "revId": "67a01027d8467b6bc48d2550a7bc8f22818cfdcd",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    }
